  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SPVO &#x2013; The WeWonder project: Socially responsible coffee farming in Uganda</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Smallholder farmers at Butta Farmers&apos; Cooperative Society Limited struggle to earn a living income from coffee cultivation. The root causes of this problem are sub-standard product quality and low yields, that negatively affect the market position of the farmers. These are largely the result of improper farming methods causing poor soil conditions, lack of biodiversity, and climate vulnerabilities.Due to insufficient farm income, the farmers can&apos;t afford to pay their farm workers a living wage. For female farm workers, especially single mothers, the situation is worst. For them, low paid farm work is the only option for generating income, as local cultural patterns don&apos;t allow single women to own land.Risk of child labour is a major consequence of the above, as the parents involved often have no other option but to share the burden with their children. The consequences are severe: denial of education opportunities, over-exploitation, exposure to hazardous conditions and sexual abuse and teenage pregnancies.To improve these conditions three independent organisations (We Wonder Company BV, Butta Farmers&apos; Cooperative Society Limited and Early Years Count Uganda) took the initiative. The project entails a comprehensive set of interventions, in synergy with local stakeholders, communities and institutions.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The proposed interventions are: 1. Improving the sustainability of coffee farming by implementing Regenerative Farming practices, in order to enhance productivity, improve quality and become resilient against climate change, midharvest&#x2019;s and pests. 2. The introduction of three specific measures targeted at fighting child labor and addressing one major root cause of it (i.e. the weak economic position of single mother farm workers): &#x2022;  The Single Women Land Owners Program, enabling single mothers to run their own farms)&#x2022;  An Early Childhood Development Center (childcare).  &#x2022;  School attendance facilitation to help remove cultural, social and financial barriers of parents to send their kids at school.3. Taking accompanying measures to secure social sustainability and ownership:&#x2022;  A social sustainability performance monitoring and audit system.&#x2022;  Introducing a grievance mechanism and appointing a confidant/counselor.  .  &#x2022;  Continuous stakeholder communication</narrative>
  </description>
